ependent path weigh on his chances", "Expert raters split between Likely R and Lean R, and markets price Ricketts around 72-73%"], "rationale": "The tied polling average rests on a thin and partisan-tinged set of surveys, while the state's fundamentals, expert ratings, and a late Republican spending wave point to a mid-single-digit Ricketts edge. Osborn's fundraising makes this a real contest, but incumbency and the red-state floor favor the incumbent.", "watch": "Whether post-mid-October polling shows Osborn holding or gaining against the Republican advertising blitz; clear independent-survey leads for him would push this toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Pete Ricketts (R) is running.", "Dan Osborn is an independent standing in for the Democratic side."]}, "overview": "Nebraska's Senate race pairs incumbent Republican Pete Ricketts with independent Dan Osborn, a challenger running on cost-of-living and populist economic themes. The state's deep Republican lean frames the contest, but surveys since late summer have shown a surprisingly close race, with several polls finding the candidates even. Nonpartisan observers place the race in the Likely or Lean Republican column, and prediction markets give Ricketts roughly a seven-in-ten chance of holding the seat.\n\nOsborn has raised about $5.8 million and holds $2.4 million on hand, while Ricketts has raised more than $11 million and benefits from national Republican groups pouring over $8 million into late advertising. A recent staff exodus has hindered Osborn's operation, yet a favorable midterm environment keeps him within striking distance. Ricketts remains favored, though likely by a narrower margin than the state's usual Republican advantage.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1905, "margin": -4.44}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1701, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.215, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2151, "margin": -5.0}, {"date"